(* ============================================================================
   PART 12: AST SERIALIZATION (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   A parsed document is exchangeable: an implementation MAY serialize its AST
   to JSON, and a consumer written against one implementation MUST be able to
   read another's output.

   1. THE SHAPE IS THE INTERCHANGE CONTRACT. An implementation whose internals
      differ maps them on output; it does not export its internal shape.

      AST TYPE NAMES FOLLOW THE PROFILE VOCABULARY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-001].
      A node's `type` string is the name profiles.md gives that node type.
      Where an implementation's own name differs, the vocabulary wins.

   1a. ADJACENT TEXT RUNS ARE COALESCED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-002]. A serialized node's
      children contain NO two adjacent `text` nodes. Where a producer's internal
      tree holds a run of them, they are joined into one before serialization,
      concatenating their `value` in order.

        foo_bar_baz and snake_case stay literal

      is ONE text node, not four. An implementation that splits a run wherever a
      delimiter failed to open emphasis publishes its parser's bookkeeping, not
      the document.

      SCOPE. The rule is about `text` nodes only, and only about ADJACENCY
      within one parent's child list. It does not merge across an intervening
      node, and it does not merge other types. It says nothing about how a
      producer builds a run while parsing -- an inline scanner that accumulates
      pieces and joins them at the end of the parse is exactly as conformant as
      one that never split.

      TEXT COALESCING OCCURS DURING `parse(x)`, NOT SERIALIZATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-003].
      PART 12 §6 requires `parse(x)` serialized and deserialized to equal `parse(x)`.
      An implementation that leaves the run split in the parsed tree and joins
      it in the encoder satisfies CARVE-P12-002 and BREAKS PART 12 §6 on the same document: what
      comes back holds one node where the tree held three, so the round trip is
      not an identity. The tree `parse(x)` returns is
      therefore already coalesced.

      POSITIONS. A merged run keeps a span only where its pieces are CONTIGUOUS
      in the source. Where they are not -- the `<` and `>` of an autolink
      unwrapped inside a link label, the delimiter and newline between two
      halves of a wrapped table cell -- the merged value is not a slice of the
      source at any offset, and PART 12 §4 rates a span that selects the wrong text
      worse than no span at all. So such a run publishes NONE.

      A run an inline include (CARVE-P6-001) assembled from more than one file
      is the exception: it spans its pieces from the file that holds the run,
      from the first one's start to the last one's end.

      `escaped_text` is NOT `text` and does not merge with it: PART 12 §3 keeps the two
      distinct on the wire because an escape is authored form, and collapsing
      them would lose the distinction the type exists to carry.

      JSON Schema cannot forbid adjacent entries of the same shape, so the
      conformance check enforces this rule as a gate.

   2. EVERY NODE IS AN OBJECT with a `type` holding the node-type identifier
      from profiles.md (CARVE-P12-001). A document is
      `{"type":"document","children":[...]}`.

      The root's fields are fixed by CARVE-P12-024. A bare tree always
      declares `astVersion: "2.0"`; `srcByteLength`, when measured, counts
      source bytes. Extension declarations, resolution stages, metadata,
      namespaced data, and the common node fields have named root slots.

      `srcByteLength` counts BYTES of the source, and is not a position
      measured under PART 12 §4. It answers how much source was read.

        {"type":"document","astVersion":"2.0","children":[...]}

   3. FIELD NAMES ARE SPEC SURFACE, exactly as kind names are. The normative
      fields per node type are those in `resources/ast-schema.json`. Primary
      content uses `children`; literal payload uses `value`. Named secondary
      content slots include `caption`, `short`, `prefix`, `locator`, `suffix`,
      `old`, `new`, `pairs`, `fallback`, `title`, and `slots`. The schema types
      each of these slots so a walker can distinguish nodes from strings.

      A `list` carries a discriminated `marker` and `spacing`, plus `children`
      of `list_item` nodes. A `table` carries row-group `children`, and each
      row carries cell `children`. A `figure` carries block `children` and a
      caption node. Authored spelling distinctions such as thematic-break
      `marker`, list marker character, and table `separatorWidths` remain
      available to a canonical writer. An implementation MUST NOT invent a
      synonym, and MUST NOT expose an
      internal field the reference does not have. A consumer reading `href`
      must not have to know which engine produced the document.

      `table.separatorWidths` records the authored separator row's dash-run
      lengths, one positive integer per separator cell, excluding alignment
      colons. An absent field means header cells were spelled with `|=`
      instead. The source writer cannot spell separator widths on a table
      without an all-header first row without promoting cells; it reports
      that field loss under CARVE-P11-046 and CARVE-P11-066. The field records
      authored spelling, even where a renderer needs none of it.

      Every typed node MAY carry `attrs`, namespaced `data`, `pos`, `segments`,
      `metadata`, `dir`, and `lang`, subject to its schema definition. The
      qualified data namespace is declared in `document.extensions`.

      A `children`-only walk reaches the primary content, while the normative
      secondary-slot table in CARVE-P12-071 completes a traversal.

      GENERATED NONBREAKING SPACES ARE NODES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-004].
      A `non_breaking_space` inline node represents one escaped space (`\ `)
      or one preserved column of line-block whitespace (CARVE-P9-044).
      It carries `type`, optional `attrs` and optional `pos`, with no value
      field. Four preserved columns are four nodes. Positions follow PART 12 §4:
      an escaped space owns its backslash and space; synthesized columns omit
      positions when expansion prevents an exact source range. This omission
      applies to the synthesized columns, not to the whole line or stanza.
      Unchanged text beside an expanded tab MUST carry its exact source span.
      A merged text value containing a synthesized space omits `pos` when no
      contiguous source slice represents it: `tab<TAB>gap` becomes `tab gap`,
      whereas the separate `a` and `b` in `a<TAB>b` each retain their spans.

      HTML emits `&nbsp;`, Markdown emits U+00A0, and plain/ANSI emit U+0020.
      Carve source writes `\ ` where that spelling survives parsing; line-block
      layout may write its preserved spaces directly. Attributes follow the
      ordinary inline-node rules. The profile policy `to_text`
      replaces this node with a text node containing U+00A0; it is separate
      from the plain-text renderer.

      ALL STRING CONTENT IS LITERAL UNICODE. U+E000 has no special meaning in
      text, code, literal-inline or raw content. A parser MUST NOT confuse an
      authored private-use character with generated whitespace. Temporary
      parser or writer markers MUST be collision-free and MUST NOT be published.

      CODE BLOCK CONTENT IS LITERAL PAYLOAD TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-064].
      `code_block.value` MUST retain every payload character, including the
      final line break when present. Container prefixes and fence delimiters
      are not payload. Source line endings use the language's LF normalization.
      A closed source fence with no payload lines has value ""; one blank
      line has "\n"; a line containing `a` has "a\n"; that line followed by
      a blank line has "a\n\n". The break before the closing fence belongs
      to the last payload line. A fence ending at EOF without a closer retains
      whether the last payload line had a break; it MUST NOT invent one.
      An unclosed fence with no payload lines also has value "".

      An imported or programmatically built code block MAY have content "a"
      without a final break. It is distinct from "a\n". JSON encoding and
      decoding MUST preserve that distinction without consulting positions,
      source text or an out-of-band flag. A reader MUST NOT guess that a
      missing final break was omitted by a producer and append one.

      HTML emits the escaped content exactly between `<code>` and `</code>`;
      it adds no payload newline. HTML import retains the code element's text,
      including whether its final break is present. Markdown emits its ordinary fenced block, adding a break before the
      closing fence only when nonempty content lacks one. The plain and ANSI
      targets emit
      the literal payload followed by their ordinary block separators. These
      three targets report no conversion diagnostic for that separator and
      MUST NOT alter the node's content.
      A canonical Carve writer needs a break before a closing fence when
      nonempty content has none. It inserts that break and reports
      `field-unspellable` for `code_block.value` on the CARVE-P11-046 channel.
      Empty content needs no inserted payload line and reports nothing.
      This report belongs to an API exposing conversion diagnostics
      (CARVE-P11-046).
      HTML import itself preserves the payload and reports no loss for a
      missing final break. An API exposing only the HTML-import report adds
      no diagnostic code for the later canonical writer's inserted break.

      RAW BLOCK CONTENT IS LITERAL PAYLOAD TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-065].
      `raw_block.value` contains the literal payload, including its authored
      final LF, under the same line-ending rules as `code_block.value`.
      An unterminated payload line contributes no final LF. An empty final
      marker line at EOF contributes no character. PART 2's outer output
      trimming does not change this interchange value. There is no separate
      payload-termination field.

      Every conversion of a raw block to a code block, including the safe
      escape in CARVE-P10-013 and profile `to_text`, uses `value` directly.
      A matching raw target omits one final LF from content containing any
      non-LF character, retaining the existing output convention; content
      consisting only of LFs retains
      all of them. This rendering convention does not alter the stored payload.
      Native formatting that adds a final payload LF MUST report
      `field-unspellable` for `raw_block.value` on CARVE-P11-046's
      conversion channel. This is separate from the render-loss report.

   3a. THE SERIALIZED TREE IS PRE-RESOLVE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-005].
      It records the authored reference key and spelling before resolution.
      A resolution result is added alongside the authored construct. For
      this reason a producer MUST NOT fold a resolution result into the tree
      in place of the authored construct.
      `[getting started][]` with no matching definition, the link carries
      `key: "getting started"`, `namespace: "link"`, and `rawRef`, but no
      `href`. A consumer MUST NOT turn it into a plain text node.

      A RESOLVED REFERENCE KEEPS ITS DESTINATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-006].
      A matching definition supplies `href` to a link or `src` to an image.
      If the definition's destination is explicitly empty, the result is the
      empty string; absence of the field means unresolved. A direct link may
      also carry an explicit empty destination and has no reference `key`.
      The old `resolved` boolean is not serialized. `rawRef` preserves the
      authored reference spelling, including collapsed empty brackets.

      For `[getting started][]` without a definition, the wire keeps the
      unresolved use as a link rather than reverting it to source text:

        {"type":"link","key":"getting started","namespace":"link",
         "rawRef":"[getting started][]"}

      With `[getting started]: /start`, the same node also has `href: "/start"`.
      If the definition's destination is empty, it has `href: ""`. This
      distinction survives JSON round trips without the old `resolved` flag.
      The collapsed form's key is the label used for resolution, while
      `rawRef` keeps the empty brackets. When inline markup appears inside
      the label, the key still records the resolved string rather than the
      author's markup ([carve#962](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/962)).

      A link target MAY declare `targetKind`: `url`, `wiki`, `doc`, or `xref`.
      A non-URL target need not pretend to be an unresolved URL. Rendering
      or conversion uses the target kind and reports a loss if it cannot
      preserve the meaning.

      A NESTED LINK AND AN AUTOLINK STAY NODES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-007].
      "Links never nest" binds the renderer, not the encoder. A link or an
      autolink inside a link's label is serialized as the node the author
      wrote, and every renderer unwraps it at the render seam. `[[x](y)](z)`
      published as a link to `z` whose only child is the text `x` has lost
      `y` and fails the PART 12 §6 round trip. THE NODE CARRIES NO NON-ANCHOR FLAG:
      consumers infer unwrapping from context.

      A DESTINATION IS THE AUTHOR'S TEXT, UNSANITIZED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-008]. `href`,
      `src`, and every other destination field carry what the author wrote,
      including a URL whose scheme no rendered URL sink is allowed to emit. A
      producer MUST NOT blank or rewrite a destination on the way into the
      tree. So this document

        [click](javascript:alert(1))

      publishes `{"type":"link","href":"javascript:alert(1)"}`. Presentation
      targets that emit URL sinks blank the destination; canonical Carve source
      preserves it under PART 11's round-trip invariant.

      A CONSUMER THAT RENDERS A DESTINATION OWNS THE DENYLIST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-009]. The
      URL-scheme denylist of CARVE-P9-054 binds every target that emits a
      resolvable URL. A consumer that wires a serialized `href` into an anchor
      applies it itself; feeding the tree back through a conforming engine
      applies it at that engine's presentation targets, and its canonical
      source writer preserves destinations.

      A CROSSREF REMAINS AN XREF NODE AFTER RESOLUTION -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-010]. `</#id>` produces `xref` with `key: "id"`,
      `namespace: "heading"`, and `form` identifying number, page, title,
      or full display. Where resolved, `href` is added. An unresolved xref
      omits `href` but keeps its key. `xref` also carries references to
      figures, tables, equations, and footnotes imported from other formats;
      the namespace tells a reader which target space to resolve.

      A renderer derives display content from the resolved target according
      to `form`. It MUST NOT replace an authored xref with a plain link during
      serialization.

   4. POSITIONS ARE REQUIRED. Every node EXCEPT the document root carries
      `pos`:

        {"startLine":1,"endLine":1,"startColumn":2,"endColumn":5,
         "startOffset":1,"endOffset":4}

      Lines and columns are 1-based. Columns count Unicode codepoints.
      Offsets are zero-based UTF-8 bytes. `endColumn` and `endOffset` are exclusive.
      `pos.file`, when present, is the canonical id of the included input the
      coordinates are measured in (CARVE-P6-001); absent means the top-level
      input. The root is exempt because it spans the whole source.

      An implementation MAY gate position tracking behind a parse option, and
      MUST enable it when asked to serialize: serialized JSON carries
      positions. One that cannot yet produce them MUST NOT emit `pos` with
      invented values and MUST NOT omit it silently; it does not yet serialize
      conformantly, and should say so.

      The span rules are four statements: what a span contains, where it
      starts, where it ends, and when a node has none.

      (1) CONTAINMENT.

      A SPAN CONTAINS ITS CHILDREN'S SPANS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-011]. A child's
      `startOffset` is not before its parent's and its `endOffset` is not past
      it, when both positions name the same input file. A child that omits
      `pos` is skipped; the comparison is against the nearest ancestor with a
      position in the same file. Containment is checked on its own, not derived
      from CARVE-P12-013. Sibling spans do not overlap, except as a PAIR: two
      breaks meeting at one line terminator share it, and a hoisted definition
      may overlap the container it was authored in (CARVE-P12-016).

      A HOISTED DEFINITION MAY CLAIM SOURCE INSIDE THE CONTAINER IT WAS
      AUTHORED IN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-016]. A definition hoisted to the
      document (CARVE-P12-029) keeps the `pos` it was written at, so it and its
      former host are siblings whose spans may overlap, whatever the host's
      extent:

          > [f]: ~
          /

      spans the emptied `block_quote` as `> ` and the definition over the whole
      line. The exception covers that pair only: two definitions claiming the
      same source, or a definition overlapping a sibling it was not authored
      in, are findings. The host is the sibling whose span begins at or before
      the definition's and which holds a child list; a
      `link_reference_definition` or `abbreviation_def` hosts nothing, a
      `footnote` does. The exception never extends a host's span over source
      no child of it owns.

      (2) WHERE A SPAN STARTS.

      A SPAN COVERS THE MARKUP THE AUTHOR WROTE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-012]. A node's span
      covers its own markup, not only its content. A `hard_break` spans its
      backslash and its newline. A trailing attribute block is the node's own
      markup: `*x*{#i}` gives `strong` offsets 0..7. A break the parser
      synthesized (a line block's implied break, a hard-break fence's converted
      newline) spans the bare newline, because the source holds no backslash.

      A SPAN BEGINS AT THE CONSTRUCT'S OPENING MARKUP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-013]. `startOffset`
      selects the markup that opened the node: the `>` of a block quote, the
      `#` of a heading, a list marker, the `:` of a colon fence, the `[` of a
      link, the backtick run of a code block. Leading indentation on the
      opening line is inside the span. Two exceptions: the inner node of the
      combined `/*x*/` form spans its content, and a table cell spans the text
      between its pipes, since the `|` opens the row.

      A CONTAINER STARTS AT ITS OPENING MARKUP EVEN WHERE ITS FIRST CHILD IS
      UNPLACED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-019]. A container whose first child
      omits `pos` starts where its opening markup was written, not at its first
      placed child.

      A SPAN DOES NOT BEGIN AT A LINE TERMINATOR -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-021]. Except on a
      `soft_break` or a `hard_break`, whose content IS the terminator, a node's
      `startOffset` MUST NOT select a newline.

      (3) WHERE A SPAN ENDS.

      A SPAN ENDS IMMEDIATELY AFTER THE CONSTRUCT'S LAST OWNED SOURCE
      CODEPOINT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-014]. Closing delimiters and trailing
      attribute blocks are included; a following line terminator, blank line or
      unattached attribute block (PART 9 §15 A4) is not. A container ends after
      its explicit closer when it has one, otherwise after its last PLACED
      child. A soft or hard break owns its line terminator, so its exclusive end
      is column 1 of the next line.

      A CONTAINER ENDS AT THE MARKUP THAT CLOSES IT EVEN WHERE ITS LAST CHILD
      IS UNPLACED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-020]. A container whose last child
      omits `pos` ends at its closing markup. A closerless container whose last
      child is unplaced ends where its content ends, which the block layer
      knows even where that text has no source slice, as for the stanza here:

          ::: |
          %%
          tab<TAB>gap
          :::

      A HOISTED SIBLING IS NOT A CHILD, AND A CLOSERLESS CONTAINER ENDS BEFORE
      IT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-015]. A span follows the tree. A definition
      hoisted out of a closerless container is its sibling, so the container
      ends at its last placed child:

          - a

            [r]: /u

      The `list` ends at offset 3, where its `list_item` ends, not at 14. A
      container with a closer still ends at the closer; CARVE-P12-016 covers
      the overlap that leaves.

      A DEFINITION LIST ENDS AT ITS LAST PLACED CHILD -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-017]. It has no
      closer, so it ends at its last placed `definition_term` or
      `definition_description`:

          :: t
          : d
            {.k}
          tail

      The `definition_list` ends where the description ends, not on the
      attribute line below it.

      A CONTAINER WITH NO PLACED CHILD AT ALL SPANS ITS OWN MARKUP AND STOPS
      THERE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-018]. A container emptied by hoisting
      (CARVE-P12-029) spans the markup that opened it, on its first line. It is
      not zero-width and does not reach to the extent the author typed:

          * * [d]: u
           :

      The inner `list_item` spans `* ` and ends there.

      (4) WHEN `pos` IS OMITTED.

      A REASSEMBLED NODE MUST OMIT `pos` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-022]. A node whose value is not
      a contiguous slice of the source, because the producer joined it from
      pieces the source separates or synthesized it outright, MUST omit `pos`
      rather than invent one, and is CONFORMANT doing so. A span over the first
      fragment misdescribes the extent and one from first to last fragment
      overlaps siblings; neither is an alternative. This covers a line block's
      synthesized hard break, line-block content rebuilt around an indentation
      sentinel, a table row or cell joined from continuation lines, and a merged
      `text` run (CARVE-P12-002) whose pieces are not adjacent:

          |= Category       |= Item   |
          | Fresh Fruits    | Apple   |
          + from local      |         |
          + farms           |         |

      The test is whether a true span EXISTS. A node the producer has not
      placed yet is a gap, not this case, and the exemption does not license
      joining more than the language requires. The conformance checker reports
      an absent `pos` because it cannot tell the two apart from the tree, so
      the position half of that check is a report and the CARVE-P12-002 half a gate.

   ============================================================================ *)

(* BEGIN AST FALLBACK MATRIX
   PART 12 INTERCHANGE FALLBACKS. These rows summarize the cited clauses,
   which prevail if the summary disagrees.
   The listed wrapper/field forms have no Carve 0.1 core source spelling;
   a parser does not synthesize those forms. Brackets restrict a row's scope.
   Algorithms, attribute preservation, diagnostic targets, API conditions
   and reporting strength remain in the cited clauses. The diagnostic column
   names known codes with the targets named by the clauses; clause leaves the code/channel
   to the cited clauses (including cross-clause channel assignments).
   Unlisted diagnostic targets and unspecified action cells add no ruling.

| Shape | Rule | Carve | HTML | Markdown | Plain | ANSI | Diagnostic |
| caption.short | CARVE-P12-033 | omit_field | ignore_field | unspecified | ignore_field | ignore_field | clause |
| figure.children[table] | CARVE-P12-036 | table_caption | figure | unspecified | unspecified | unspecified | clause |
| table_cell.children | CARVE-P12-049 | unspecified | cell_blocks | flatten_blocks | flatten_blocks | flatten_blocks | field-unspellable (Carve,Markdown,Plain,ANSI) |
| small_caps | CARVE-P12-050 | unwrap | smallcaps_span | smallcaps_span | unwrap | unwrap | structure-unspellable (Carve) |
| section | CARVE-P12-052 | unwrap | section | unwrap | unwrap | unwrap | structure-unspellable (Carve) |
| ruby | CARVE-P12-054 | parenthesized_pairs | ruby | ruby | parenthesized_pairs | parenthesized_pairs | ruby-flattened (Carve,Plain,ANSI) |
| block_extension[unknown name] | CARVE-P12-055 | fallback | fallback | fallback | fallback | fallback | clause |

   ACTIONS (details and exceptions remain in the cited clause):
   omit_field: omit the field.
   ignore_field: ignore the field; render the full caption once.
   unspecified: not specified by this row.
   table_caption: write the table and one caption slot (PART 12 §17).
   figure: figure around table with outer figcaption.
   flatten_blocks: flatten blocks into one cell line (PART 12 §27).
   cell_blocks: render blocks in td/th.
   unwrap: render children without this wrapper.
   smallcaps_span: span class="smallcaps".
   section: section element.
   parenthesized_pairs: base(annotation) in pair order.
   ruby: ruby with rt and generated rp.
   fallback: render the declared fallback (PART 12 §33); the row applies only when
     the reader does not know the extension name and the envelope does not
     mark it required.
   END AST FALLBACK MATRIX *)
